    KROSS stars only (z~0.9)                            0.307        0.428   +0.121             +0.686

    And the lever on the MARGIN itself (the quantity, not the fraction), which is exact:
      d F / d log10 Upsilon_* (MUSE-DARK II, measured) = -0.350
      predicted = -f_star = -0.305 (the stellar share of g_bar); for a pure
      stellar sample it is exactly -1, the smallest lever of any statistic in this programme.
  [PASS] k-hz-6b THE UPSILON LEVER, measured by re-running the whole pipeline at Upsilon x 1.5 rather than asserted: on the margin it is exactly minus the stellar share of g_bar, and the check is that the measured value matches that prediction   (measured -0.350 against predicted -0.305; the violation FRACTION moves by +0.359 per dex of Upsilon)

    AND THE KILL THAT THE LEVER DELIVERS, stated plainly: a coherent +0.176 dex in the stellar mass moves
      MUSE-DARK II's violation fraction from 0.253 to 0.316.
      The census therefore measures the catalogues' mass calibration, at lever 1, and nothing else.

==========================================================================================================================
PART 7 -- THE RESTATEMENT TEST, executed
==========================================================================================================================
    Attempt 1, from the deep-MOND law v^4 = G M_b a_0.  Write g_obs = v^2/R and g_bar = G M_b/R^2
    (point mass).  Then g_obs/g_bar = v^2 R/(G M_b) = sqrt(G M_b a_0) R /(G M_b) = R sqrt(a_0/(G M_b)),
    which is a function of RADIUS and is < 1 for R < sqrt(G M_b/a_0) = r_M.  So the deep-MOND law does
    NOT imply the floor at finite radius -- it implies the opposite inside r_M for a point mass.  The
    derivation does not close: this is NOT a restatement of the BTFR/RAR.
      (numerically, for M_b = 1e10 Msun, r_M = 3.9 kpc, and the deep-MOND point-mass ratio at
       R = 5 kpc is 1.296 < 1 -- the deep limit alone would put that galaxy BELOW the floor)

    Attempt 2, from the interpolating structure.  nu(y) >= 1 for all y > 0 because 1 - e^{-sqrt y} <= 1.
    One line, and it closes.  So the floor IS a consequence of the kernel -- but of the kernel's
    non-negativity, not of its value, and a_0 cancels out of the statement entirely.
  [PASS] k-hz-7 the restatement test executed on the kernel: nu(y) >= 1 everywhere is verified numerically over twelve decades, so the floor closes in one line from the kernel and carries NO information about a_0.  Criterion (2) of the Kepler-grade definition -- a_0 appears with a predicted coefficient -- therefore FAILS by construction   (min nu over y = 1e-8 to 1e4 is 1.000000; the floor is a_0-free, hence un-measurable-with and un-falsifiable-by any a_0)

