PART 8 -- what the census DOES buy: the one-sided censoring correction to every published high-z a_0
==========================================================================================================================
    sample                                        a_0 of inverting subset  censored whole-sample median  shift (dex)
    MUSE-DARK II lensed (z 0.5-1.45)                            1.102e-10                     5.116e-11       -0.333
    RC100 (z 0.6-2.5)                                           2.117e-10                     1.520e-10       -0.144
    MSA-3D golden, stars only (z 0.6-1.7)                       1.933e-09                     1.933e-09       +0.000
    KROSS stars only (z~0.9)                                    4.012e-10                     1.507e-10       -0.425

    Every published high-z a_0 level is the median of the INVERTING subset.  Because the censored
    galaxies are, by definition, the ones with the smallest g_obs/g_bar, they belong at the BOTTOM of
    the a_0 distribution: the published levels are upper-leaning by the shift above.
  [PASS] k-hz-8 THE ONE THING THE CENSUS IS GOOD FOR, and it is a correction rather than a law: placing the non-inverting galaxies below all others moves MUSE-DARK II's level by a definite, large amount.  This check fails if the censoring is negligible   (see the table; the shift is reported per survey and is the operative caveat on every high-z a_0 quoted in this programme)

