PART 9 -- mutation controls
==========================================================================================================================
  [PASS] M1 SHUFFLE: permuting the baryonic masses across MUSE-DARK II must change the violation fraction; if it does not, the census is measuring the sample's marginal distributions and not the pairing   (real 0.253 vs shuffled 0.369 +- 0.029)
    M_bar x 0.01 : violation fraction 0.000  (0 of 95)
    M_bar x 0.1  : violation fraction 0.011  (1 of 95)
    M_bar x 0.5  : violation fraction 0.105  (10 of 95)
    M_bar x 2.0  : violation fraction 0.547  (52 of 95)
  [PASS] M2 CLOSURE, in the limit: as the baryonic mass goes to zero the violation fraction must go to zero, because the floor becomes trivially satisfiable.  If it does not, the estimator is broken   (M_bar/100 gives 0 violations out of 95; at M_bar/10 ONE galaxy still violates (AS1063 952, 1.17 dex below its own floor with 97% of its mass from the HI scaling relation) -- reported rather than thresholded away, because a single object no mass rescaling can save is a data pathology, not a measurement of gravity)
    gas scale length x 1.0 : violation fraction 0.253   (more extended gas encloses less at 2 R_e, so the floor is easier to satisfy)
    gas scale length x 1.5 : violation fraction 0.211   (more extended gas encloses less at 2 R_e, so the floor is easier to satisfy)
    gas scale length x 2.0 : violation fraction 0.158   (more extended gas encloses less at 2 R_e, so the floor is easier to satisfy)
  [PASS] M3 GEOMETRY STRESS, against interest: the census depends on an assumption nobody measured for these galaxies -- the gas scale length.  Doubling it changes the violation fraction, so the 25% is a statement about the assumed gas distribution as much as about the galaxies   (gas x1 0.253 vs gas x2 0.158)
  [PASS] M4 KERNEL SWAP: the floor holds for the 'simple' interpolation function too (nu = 0.5 + sqrt(0.25 + 1/y)), so nothing here is specific to Route A -- again showing the statistic cannot discriminate between kernels either   (min simple-nu over five decades = 1.0099)

==========================================================================================================================
VERDICT (k-high-z floor census, candidate K-C)
==========================================================================================================================
  DEAD.  NOT A SECOND LAW, and it dies twice over -- once on the definition and once on the data.
  Two checks FAIL below and BOTH failures are the result; rc = 1 is the intended outcome.

  ON THE DEFINITION
   * Criterion (2) fails BY CONSTRUCTION: a_0 does not appear in g_obs >= g_bar, so no coefficient
     can be predicted and none can be measured.  Part 6 proves the footing-independence the
     proposal claimed as a strength -- and it is the same fact that disqualifies it.
   * Criterion (1)/(4) fail too: Part 5 shows LambdaCDM predicts the identical floor, because
     non-negative dark mass plus circular motion is all the derivation needs.  It is not the
     framework's statement and nobody would credit it to the framework.
   * The restatement test (Part 7): the floor does NOT follow from v^4 = G M_b a_0 -- the deep limit
     alone puts a point mass BELOW the floor inside r_M -- but it follows in one line from nu >= 1.
     Not a restatement of the BTFR; a restatement of positivity.

  ON THE DATA -- and this is the part the proposing stage did not have
   * The 25.3% violation fraction is NOT in excess of the catalogue's own errors: expected 22.8%,
     observed 25.3%, 0.65 sigma (k-hz-2, FAIL against the pre-specified >2 sigma gate).
   * The z = 0 estimator control says most of it is the estimator: the SAME 139 SPARC galaxies
     violate 0.7% of the time with resolved photometry and 18.0% when degraded to (M_bar, R_e, one
     velocity).  17.3 of the 25.3 points are reproduced at z = 0 with no redshift at all.
   * The violators are not random: violation fraction runs 1.000 / 0.316 / 0.190 / 0.047 across
     bins of increasing stellar share f_star, corr(f_star, violation) = -0.52.  Every galaxy whose
     catalogued mass is >95% scaling-relation gas violates; almost none whose mass is >35% stars.
   * And the redshift census dies with it: the raw +3.28 +- 0.98 logistic slope (3.3 sigma) falls to
     +1.12 +- 0.90 (1.2 sigma) once the stellar share is partialled out of z (k-hz-3a, FAIL).  It is
     k03's own gas-fraction artefact again, arriving through a different statistic.

  WHAT SURVIVES, and it is worth keeping
   * A data-quality meter with the smallest lever in the programme, MEASURED at Upsilon x 1.5:
     d F/d log Upsilon = -0.350 against a predicted -f_star = -0.305.  For a star-dominated sample
     it is exactly -1, against |LAMBDA| = 1.5-3.4 for a_0 itself.
   * A one-sided censoring correction every published high-z a_0 needs: -0.33 dex (MUSE-DARK II),
     -0.14 (RC100), -0.43 (KROSS).  Published high-z a_0 levels are upper-leaning by that much.

  AND ONE RESULT AGAINST MY OWN EXPECTATION.  I expected Part 4 to damage item 16.  It does not:
  RC100's two independent routes to g_bar -- its tabulated f_DM and its tabulated M_bar with the
  exact disc geometry -- agree to +0.010 dex in the median, and the resulting a_0 to +0.034 dex,
  well inside the 0.10 dex gate.  What they do NOT share is the tails: the scatter between routes is
  0.219 dex, and 22 of 100 RC100 galaxies that HAVE an a_0 on the f_DM route have none at all on the
  mass route.  Item 16's level is validated; its per-galaxy values are not interchangeable.
==========================================================================================================================

RESULT: 18 checks, 2 FAIL -> ["k-hz-2 THE NOISE-FORWARD TEST, and it is the one that decides whether the census is a finding: MUSE-DARK II's violation fraction is compared with what its OWN quoted errors produce when the floor holds exactly.  The check fails if the observed fraction is NOT in excess of the noise", "k-hz-3a THE CONTROL THE DIAGNOSIS DEMANDS: the census's redshift trend is re-fitted with the stellar share of g_bar partialled out of z.  The check is that a trend claimed as a redshift effect survives removing the one variable that is known to run with z by construction in these catalogues (the gas scaling relations)"]   rc=1
